To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we use the formula:

ΔMT = Σ[H2O(f) of reactants] - Σ[H2O(f) of products]

Where:
- ΔMT is the molar heat of reaction
- H2O(f) is the heat of formation

For the given reaction:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

The atomic heat of reaction is:

ΔMT = H2O(f)[Ca(OH)2(s)] - H2O(f)[CaO(s)] - H2O(f)[H2O(l)]

Substituting the given values:

ΔMT = (-986.2 kg/mol) - (-635.1 kg/mol) - (-285.8 kg/mol)
ΔMT = -986.2 + 635.1 + 285.8
ΔMT = -155.3 kg/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction is $\boxed{-155.3 \text{ kg/mol}}$ .
