","The change of enthalpy for the reaction is calculated using the following formula:

\[ \Delta S = \sum S_{\text{exp}} - \sum S_{\text{obs}} \]

Where:
- \( S_{\text{exp}} \) is the molar solubility of the experimental species
- \( S_{\text{obs}} \) is the molar solubility of the observed substances

For the given reaction:
\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) \]

The molar solubilities are:
- \( S_{\text{PbS}} = 91.2 \, \text{J/K mol} \)
- \( S_{\text{O}_2} = 205.1 \, \text{J/K mol} \)
- \( S_{\text{PolO}} = 66.5 \, \text{GM/K mol} \)
- \( S_{\text{SO}_2} = 248.2 \, \text{GM/K mol} \)

Substituting the molar solubilities into the formula:

\[ \Delta S = (2 \times 66.5 + 2 \times 248.2) - (2 \times 91.2 + 3 \times 205.1) \]
\[ \Delta S = (132 + 496.4) - (182.4 + 615.3) \]
