","Since $f(x)=\sin (\omega x+\varphi )$, we have $f(0)=\sin (\omega \cdot 0+\varphi )=\sin \varphi$.

Given $f(0)=-f( \frac{\pi}{2} )$, we have $\sin \phi=-\sin \left( \omega \cdot \frac{\pi}{2}+\varphi \right)$.

Since $\sin \varphi=-\sin \left( \omega \frac{\pi}{2}+\varphi \right)$, we have $\sin \varphi=\sin \left( -\omega \frac{\pi}{2}-\varphi \right)$.

This implies $\varphi=-\omega \frac{\pi}{2}-\varphi+2k\pi$ or $\varphi=\-\omega \frac{\pi}{2}-\varphi+2k\pi$ for some integer $k$.

